.TH du 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
du \- estimate file space usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY du
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.RI [ FILE \~.\|.\|.\&]
.YS
.SY du
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.BI \-\-files0\-from= F
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Summarize device usage of the set of FILEs, recursively for directories.
.PP
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-0
\fB\-0, \-\-null\fP
.UE
end each output line with NUL, not newline
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-a
\fB\-a, \-\-all\fP
.UE
write counts for all files, not just directories
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-A
\fB\-A, \-\-apparent\-size\fP
.UE
print apparent sizes rather than device usage;
although the apparent size is usually smaller, it may be
larger due to holes in ('sparse') files,
internal fragmentation, indirect blocks, etc.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-B
\fB\-B, \-\-block\-size=\,SIZE\fP
.UE
scale sizes by SIZE before printing them; See SIZE format below;
E.g., '\-BM' prints sizes in units of 1,048,576 bytes
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-b
\fB\-b, \-\-bytes\fP
.UE
equivalent to '\-\-apparent\-size \fB\-\-block\-size\fR=\fI\,1'\/\fR
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-c
\fB\-c, \-\-total\fP
.UE
produce a grand total
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-D
\fB\-D, \-\-dereference\-args\fP
.UE
dereference only symlinks that are listed on the command line
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-d
\fB\-d, \-\-max\-depth=\,N\fP
.UE
print the total for a directory (or file, with \fB\-\-all\fR)
only if it is N or fewer levels below the command
line argument;  \fB\-\-max\-depth\fR=\fI\,0\/\fR is the same as \fB\-\-summarize\fR
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-files0\-from
\fB\-\-files0\-from=F\fP
.UE
summarize device usage of the NUL\-terminated file names
specified in file F;  if F is \-, read names from standard input
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-H
\fB\-H\fP
.UE
equivalent to \fB\-\-dereference\-args\fR (\fB\-D\fR)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-h
\fB\-h, \-\-human\-readable\fP
.UE
print sizes in human readable format (e.g., 1K 234M 2G)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-inodes
\fB\-\-inodes\fP
.UE
list inode usage information instead of block usage
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-k
\fB\-k\fP
.UE
like \fB\-\-block\-size\fR=\fI\,1K\/\fR
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-L
\fB\-L, \-\-dereference\fP
.UE
dereference all symbolic links
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-l
\fB\-l, \-\-count\-links\fP
.UE
count sizes many times if hard linked
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-m
\fB\-m\fP
.UE
like \fB\-\-block\-size\fR=\fI\,1M\/\fR
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-P
\fB\-P, \-\-no\-dereference\fP
.UE
don't follow any symbolic links (this is the default)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-S
\fB\-S, \-\-separate\-dirs\fP
.UE
for directories do not include size of subdirectories
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-si
\fB\-\-si\fP
.UE
like \fB\-h\fR, but use powers of 1000 not 1024
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-s
\fB\-s, \-\-summarize\fP
.UE
display only a total for each argument
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-t
\fB\-t, \-\-threshold=\,SIZE\fP
.UE
exclude entries smaller than SIZE if positive,
or entries greater than SIZE if negative
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-time
\fB\-\-time\fP
.UE
show time of the last modification of any file in the directory,
or any of its subdirectories
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-time
\fB\-\-time=WORD\fP
.UE
show time as WORD instead of modification time:
atime, access, use, ctime or status
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-time\-style
\fB\-\-time\-style=STYLE\fP
.UE
time/date format with \fB\-\-time\fR; see TIME_STYLE below
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-X
\fB\-X, \-\-exclude\-from=\,FILE\fP
.UE
exclude files that match any pattern in FILE
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-\-exclude
\fB\-\-exclude=PATTERN\fP
.UE
exclude files that match PATTERN
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#du\-x
\fB\-x, \-\-one\-file\-system\fP
.UE
skip directories on different file systems
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/du#du\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/du#du\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
Display values are in units of the first available SIZE from \fB\-\-block\-size\fR,
and the DU_BLOCK_SIZE, BLOCK_SIZE and BLOCKSIZE environment variables.
Otherwise, units default to 1024 bytes (or 512 if POSIXLY_CORRECT is set).
.PP
The SIZE argument is an integer and optional unit (example: 10K is 10*1024).
Units are K,M,G,T,P,E,Z,Y,R,Q (powers of 1024) or KB,MB,... (powers of 1000).
Binary prefixes can be used, too: KiB=K, MiB=M, and so on.
.PP
The \fB\-\-time\-style\fR STYLE argument can be full\-iso, long\-iso, iso, or +FORMAT.
FORMAT is interpreted like in \fBdate\fP(1).
Also the TIME_STYLE environment variable sets the default style to use.
.SH PATTERNS
PATTERN is a shell pattern (not a regular expression).  The pattern
.B ?\&
matches any one character, whereas
.B *
matches any string (composed of zero, one or multiple characters).  For
example,
.B *.o
will match any files whose names end in
.BR .o .
Therefore, the command
.IP
.B du \-\-exclude=\(aq*.o\(aq
.PP
will skip all files and subdirectories ending in
.B .o
(including the file
.B .o
itself).
